  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: `append(xs, [x])` is accepted and RUN by the seed interpreter as a concatenation, while std.algebra's collection roster row `append` is [ReceiverSelf, ReceiverElement] -> ReceiverSelf, an ELEMENT push, and the v2 native route types it that way. The same source therefore means two things: gunbc.guarantee_measurement distinct_string_count (`fold(values, init: [], f: fn(acc, v) { if any(acc, e => e == v) { acc } else { append(acc, [v]) } })`) collapsed duplicates under the seed (claim_batch PASS on test.claim.distinct_string_count_witness at 3b73ef0f6611, srv1) while the native emission typed the accumulator Rc<Vec<Rc<Vec<String>>>> and rustc refused E0308 at 239:172 and 242:52 (calm-crab-645, srv1 q-052e584/e0308-rebuild.txt). HARM: code the seed admits breaks on v2 at rustc; the refusal is loud, but the author cannot see it on the v1 route, and v1 is the route most claims run on.",
    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS: `list_append(left, right)` (std.algebra) takes two lists and is the concat spelling; `append` is the element spelling. The `e == v` in the same site compared List<String> with String and was admitted on the native route as well (see comparison_operands_never_judged_in_v2_infer; the operand type of a callback parameter drawn from an unclosed fold carrier is not derived). A second site with the identical text was gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_identity distinct_count. Both rewritten to concat(acc, [v]), which both routes agree on.",
